+### Byte Sizes
+
+The {class}`ByteSize` type converts a size such as `512`, `10MB`, `1.5 GiB` or `2k` to a number of bytes. Units of
+1000 (`KB`, `MB`, `GB`, `TB`, or just `K`, `M`, `G`, `T`) and of 1024 (`KiB`, `MiB`, `GiB`, `TiB`) are accepted in any
+letter case. `min` and `max` work as in {class}`IntRange` and can be sizes too.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--cache", type=click.ByteSize(max="1GiB"), default=10 * 1024**2, show_default=True)
+    def serve(cache):
+        click.echo(f"cache: {cache} bytes")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(serve, args=["--help"])
+    invoke(serve, args=["--cache", "64MiB"])
+```
+
+The help shows the default and the range in binary units, `[default: 10 MiB; x<=1 GiB]`, and so does a prompt for a
+size. A custom type can show its values the same way by overriding {meth}`ParamType.format_value`.
+
 ## How to Implement Custom Types
 
 To implement a custom type, you need to subclass the {class}`ParamType` class. For simple cases, passing a Python
 function that fails with a `ValueError` is also supported, though discouraged. Override the {meth}`~ParamType.convert`
 method to convert the value from a string to the correct type.
